distroforge (0.3.5-4) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Stage the UEFI amorce for --from-scratch ISOs, which had none at all.
    create_iso_tree built a BIOS El Torito image and stopped there, so an
    amd64 ISO booted only under legacy firmware while an arm64 one carried
    no boot record whatsoever -- the "boots EFI-only" marker described media
    no machine can start, and xorriso exited 0 either way. The ESP is now
    assembled with mtools from the target rootfs's own shim and signed GRUB
    (new Depends: mtools), which adds no package to the delivered image:
    shim-signed already depends on grub-efi-<arch>-signed, and
    grub-efi-<arch>-bin on grub-efi-<arch>-unsigned.
  * Stop passing -isohybrid-gpt-basdat with no -isohybrid-mbr to pair it
    with, and open the El Torito EFI entry through --efi-boot so the BIOS
    entry's -boot-load-size cannot bleed into it. Neither was reachable
    until the change above produced an EFI image for the first time.
  * Install grub-efi-ia32-bin on i386 rather than grub-efi-i386-bin, which
    has no candidate in any archive, so every --bootstrap-arch i386 build
    died at apt-get install.
  * Refuse to build an ISO from a tree carrying no boot image for either
    firmware, and report missing mtools at validation rather than an hour
    into the build.
  * Write the GRUB trampoline where a signed GRUB actually looks. Its prefix is
    compiled in and cannot be re-set -- Ubuntu's is /EFI/ubuntu, and the binary
    carries no embedded configuration, only the "%s/grub.cfg" lookup -- so the
    copy in EFI/BOOT was never read. Measured on a real OVMF boot: shim loaded,
    GRUB started, and the machine sat at a rescue "grub>" prompt. Both the ESP
    and the ISO tree now carry it at EFI/<family>/grub.cfg as well.
  * Stop debranding EFI/<vendor> directory names. That name is a firmware
    contract, not branding: renaming EFI/ubuntu to the product name left the
    signed bootloader looking for a directory that no longer existed, which is
    what produced the rescue prompt above. Branded names inside the directory
    are still renamed.
  * Exclude the pseudo-filesystem contents from mksquashfs, and refuse to pack a
    rootfs that still has anything mounted under it. A runtime bind mount that
    survived its unmount made mksquashfs walk into /proc/kcore, whose apparent
    size is the whole 128 TiB address space: a 2.0 GB rootfs produced 5.7 GB of
    squashfs in thirty minutes and was still growing when it was stopped. The
    mount points themselves are kept, since a live system mounts onto them.
  * Bootstrap ca-certificates into the rootfs, so a from-scratch build can
    reach its archive at all. A minbase chroot has no CA store and every
    archive URL here is https, so the first in-chroot apt-get update failed TLS
    verification against every index -- while ca-certificates sat in the very
    package list that update was fetching for. Found by running the chain: the
    build died on "Unable to locate package sudo".
  * Make a failed apt-get update fatal in the chroot, through one shared
    APT_UPDATE_ARGV carrying APT::Update::Error-Mode=any. apt returns 0 when
    every index failed to download -- verified against apt 3.2.0, rc 0 without
    the option and rc 100 with it -- so the TLS failure above was swallowed and
    surfaced three phases later as a misleading package error.
  * Detect the OVMF firmware images rather than naming them. The default was
    /usr/share/OVMF/OVMF_CODE.fd in nine places, a file no ovmf package has
    shipped since the firmware moved to a 4 MB flash size, so every UEFI QEMU
    run in the product failed on a missing file. --prebuild-vm-ovmf-code and
    --prebuild-vm-ovmf-vars now default to empty, meaning auto-detect, and the
    matching GUI fields show the detected path as placeholder text.
  * Pair --prebuild-vm-secure-boot with the .secboot firmware build and the .ms
    variable store holding the enrolled keys, and refuse a named pair that
    cannot enforce Secure Boot instead of substituting one: the flag used to
    report Secure Boot on a VM running with it off. Add the first tests for
    validate_prebuild_vm_options, which had none.
  * Correct docs/platform-refactor-audit.md, which still recorded that mypy,
    pre-commit and shellcheck were "not wired anywhere" long after make check
    and CI had begun running all three.
  * Let a build choose its live-filesystem compressor, through
    --squashfs-compression and a matching Build & Release field, instead of
    taking whatever the release table names. Only compressors a kernel can
    mount are offered: mksquashfs also writes lzma, which its own man page
    marks "deprecated - no kernel support", so such an image would pack,
    checksum, pass every artifact gate, ship, and then fail to mount on the
    machine it was built for. It is now refused at validation, and a data test
    keeps the release table inside the same set. Measured on the 2.6 GB minbase
    rootfs of a real from-scratch build: xz 93 s for 1443 MiB, zstd 37 s for
    1466 MiB, lz4 3.9 s for 1657 MiB. No block size, dictionary size, BCJ
    filter or compression level is passed, also measured -- "-b 1M -Xdict-size
    100%" costs 34% more wall clock for 2.2% of the image and "-Xbcj x86"
    another 161% for 0.5%, because a live rootfs is mostly payload that already
    ships compressed. The full table is in docs/build-pipeline.md.
  * Let boot-proof choose the firmware it proves, through --firmware bios|uefi
    and --secure-boot. Its QEMU backend never set one, so it always ran BIOS and
    UEFI was reachable only through a build: on a BIOS host a green report
    confirmed the half that already worked. The report now names the firmware it
    ran, in the text and in boot-proof.json, and Secure Boot on BIOS, an OVMF
    image that is not installed and a firmware pair that cannot enforce Secure
    Boot are refused before QEMU starts rather than after it has booted the
    wrong machine. Asking for a firmware with --backend iso-scan says the choice
    did not apply instead of wearing a firmware nothing used. The GUI keeps one
    firmware control -- the Virtualization Lab combo already feeds this proof --
    and the Artifacts button says so.
  * Stop bind-mounting the host's /run into the build chroot, and give the
    target an empty tmpfs instead. The host /run holds the control sockets of
    the host's own daemons -- snapd, systemd, D-Bus, udev -- so every
    maintainer script in the target root could command the build machine as
    root. policy-rc.d closes
    only the invoke-rc.d route: measured on Ubuntu 26.04, the postinst or postrm
    of udev, dbus, snapd, polkitd, accountsservice and networkd-dispatcher each
    call "systemctl --system daemon-reload" or "systemctl try-restart" directly.
    A minbase bootstrap runs few such scripts and a desktop seed runs hundreds,
    which is how this surfaced. Nor was it hypothetical: snaps installed from a
    chroot phase had already been found landing in the build host's
    /var/lib/snapd, which is why that phase is refused. What that empty tmpfs
    then cost the target is the next three entries: it was not free, and the
    claim first written here that "apt is unaffected" held only until
    systemd-resolved was installed.
  * Give the private /run back the two things the target had put there, after a
    from-scratch desktop build died at apt-get update with "Temporary failure
    resolving". systemd-resolved's postinst points /etc/resolv.conf at
    ../run/systemd/resolve/stub-resolv.conf and copies the working content
    there, so that copy died with the phase that made it and the next phase
    mounted a fresh empty tmpfs under a dangling symlink. Every phase now
    reseeds the resolver, and only ever inside that mount: a resolver on the
    persistent rootfs is left alone, absolute link targets are read as
    <root>-relative the way the chroot's own kernel reads them, a chain is
    followed through linked directories, and only nameserver lines are copied,
    never this machine's search domain. A phase that seeds nothing records why.
    /run/lock comes back with it, mode 1777: base-files.postinst creates it
    during the bootstrap and Debian Policy makes /var/lock a symlink to it, so
    mounting over /run left /var/lock dangling from the second phase onward and
    any flock() under it failing with ENOENT.
  * Mount that tmpfs mode=0755,nosuid,nodev,noexec,size=10%, which is what this
    machine's own /run carries. A bare mount -t tmpfs comes up 1777 with suid,
    dev and exec permitted and no size cap, so the replacement was wider than
    the bind it replaced: any uid on the build machine could write into the
    target's /run for a whole phase, including over the resolver that every apt
    call in that phase reads.
  * Run the changelog's own policy tests from pre-commit when it changes. The
    entry above went out one character over the lintian line limit, because
    the changelog is written after the gate has been run, and CI answered a
    formatting tag with ten red jobs. The check now fires where the mistake
    is made.

 -- DistroForge maintainers <maintainers@distroforge.invalid>  Mon, 27 Jul 2026 08:03:10 +0200

distroforge (0.3.5-3) resolute; urgency=medium

  * Remove debian/distroforge.postinst. Its embedded python3 payload raised
    SyntaxError on every invocation from 0.3.4-4 onward, and the absence of
    #DEBHELPER# meant dh_python3's py3compile fragment was dropped, so the
    installed package never byte-compiled its modules. The generated postinst
    is restored by dropping the override.
  * Add "distroforge dock --pin/--unpin", and a dock checkbox in the First Run
    dialog, as the consenting replacement for that hook: the GNOME favorites
    are written for the invoking user, in that user's own session, without
    privilege escalation, and can be revoked the same way.
  * Grade lintian from its tags instead of its exit code, which reported a
    package carrying warnings as clean, and keep every tag in the reason. Pin
    the invocation to "--profile debian": a lintian profile is a vendor, never
    a suite. Stop requiring a lintian overrides file the package does not ship.
  * Run the test phase against the staged build tree via PYBUILD_TEST_ARGS, so
    a file missing from package-data fails the build instead of passing it
    against the source checkout.
  * Drop debootstrap, qemu-system-x86 and squashfs-tools from Build-Depends:
    the offline rootless test suite never invokes them and they are already
    runtime Depends. Drop the qemu-kvm Recommends, a virtual package with no
    candidate; doctor now names qemu-system-x86 so its apt line can be pasted.
  * Give each autopkgtest its own $AUTOPKGTEST_TMP scratch directory instead of
    fixed /tmp names, assert the packaged example rather than falling back to
    the source tree, exercise distroforge-typer, and add a GUI import test that
    declares python3-pyqt6 rather than relying on the @ expansion.
  * Reject archive members whose symlink target escapes the destination, and
    quote every argument of the generated desktop-source and printed-plan
    commands, closing three untrusted-input paths.
  * Fix the checksum manifest naming the wrong file, the chroot hooks shipped
    inside the image, the missing dpkg-deb packaging step, the unprivileged
    snapshot restore, the unversioned ISO name, the accepted all-zero GPG
    fingerprint, and the kernel selection crash on a non-numeric directory.
  * Type the tree against one Qt binding under TYPE_CHECKING while keeping the
    PySide6-then-PyQt6 fallback at runtime. The try/except shim made
    QMainWindow resolve to Any, and an Any base class made every attribute
    access legal, so the main window was never type-checked; turning it on
    surfaced 136 errors, including two window protocols whose loose
    annotations rejected the window implementing them.
  * Make --json mean one JSON document and exactly one trailing newline on
    every command that offers it. "explain" ended with a blank line, "capture"
    and "livefs-iso-plan" with none, "new --json" printed prose, and
    "livefs-iso-build --json" appended an English sentence after the closing
    brace; that advisory now goes to stderr. The GUI and the CLI also wrote
    different bytes for the same captured profile.
  * Add --artifact-dir to debian-package, evidence-status and the hermetic
    bundle, with a matching Artifacts page field. Six call sites hardcoded the
    parent of the source tree, so an archive kept elsewhere produced a clean
    report about an empty directory.
  * Clear every lintian tag that was a real defect, without adding a single
    override. Move the manual pages, desktop entry and launcher icon out of
    debian/ into share/ in the upstream tree, since upstream and the packaging
    are the same project; declare Homepage, Vcs-Git, Vcs-Browser, a DEP-12
    debian/upstream/metadata and a comments-only debian/watch recording that
    upstream publishes no tarball and no release tag, so there is nothing for
    uscan to poll; name the repository in debian/copyright instead of "local
    source tree"; drop the
    Priority field, redundant since dpkg 1.22.13. Rules-Requires-Root: no
    stays: two tests assert it, so the rootless build contract must not depend
    on which dpkg ran the build. That leaves exactly one pedantic tag, and
    debian/README.source records why.
  * Split the Current Shape table in docs/platform-refactor-audit.md into
    sections. A Markdown table row cannot be wrapped, so two cells had grown to
    1189 and 600 characters on one line.
  * Resolve the lintian profile from the suite debian/changelog targets rather
    than pinning "debian". A profile is a vendor, and the vendor also decides
    which values the .changes Distribution field may hold, so grading this
    resolute-targeted package against the Debian profile reported
    bad-distribution-in-changes-file for a correct field -- and because the
    verdict is graded from tags, DistroForge rated its own compliant package
    failed. The mapping is read from lintian's own per-vendor suite lists, and
    "debian" stays the fallback, so the verdict still cannot depend on the
    build host. The verification command printed in the hermetic bundle
    carried no profile at all, and now carries the one it was graded with.

